# XSH stpcpy() and strcpy()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Sources are in arrays (not literals) and destinations are filled with
# 'X' first, so that bytes written past the terminator show. A
# destination of exactly the string's size is also placed so that it
# ends where an inaccessible (PROT_NONE) page begins.

assertion  XSH.stpcpy.001
anchor     functions/stpcpy.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/stpcpy.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2008
says       stpcpy() of "", "a" and a 300-byte string copies the string and
           its null byte into s1, changes no byte after it, and returns
           s1 + strlen(s2); into a 6-byte array that ends at an
           inaccessible page, stpcpy() of "hello" works the same.
test       t/001-stpcpy.c

assertion  XSH.stpcpy.002
anchor     functions/stpcpy.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/stpcpy.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       strcpy() of the same strings copies the string and its null byte
           into s1, changes no byte after it, and returns s1, also into the
           6-byte array at an inaccessible page.
test       t/002-strcpy.c

assertion  XSH.stpcpy.003
anchor     functions/stpcpy.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       stpcpy() and strcpy() leave errno unchanged, both when it is 0
           and when it is EDOM.
test       t/003-errno-kept.c
